<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        This is the theme Capacitor forces onto the Activity from
        BridgeActivity.onCreate via setTheme(R.style.AppTheme_NoActionBar), so it
        decides what the native window looks like for the whole session.

        The parent was Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar, which resolves
        colorBackground to near-black when the device is in night mode. The app is
        entirely light (there is no prefers-color-scheme CSS anywhere in
        src/styles.css), so pinning Light matches reality and makes a dark window
        background impossible.

        android:background used to be @null, which is what caused the visible blink.
        Measured at 60fps on a cold launch, the sequence was:

            splash 950ms -> PURE BLACK 233ms -> splash 1583ms -> app

        The platform's starting window has an exit animation and fades out over the
        Activity window. With the decor background set to @null there was nothing
        behind that fade, so it revealed black - a 233ms black flash in the middle
        of the splash, which is exactly the reported blink.

        Giving the window the same branded artwork as the starting window means the
        fade now reveals the identical splash instead of black, so the hand-off is
        invisible. It is only ever visible before the WebView paints; the document
        puts an opaque bg-slate-50 on <body> in its initial markup, so once the app
        renders this cannot show through.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@drawable/splash_window</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/splash_window</item>
        <item name="android:forceDarkAllowed">false</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        Launch theme.

        The parent was Theme.SplashScreen (androidx). On API < 31 that compat
        layer replaces android:windowBackground with its own composition built
        from windowSplashScreenBackground, and on this device that produced a
        completely BLANK white starting window - verified on camera by blocking
        the WebView's first paint, which showed 96.9% white and zero TCS pixels.
        So the launch ran plain white -> then the web #boot overlay with the
        logo and bar, which is the double splash that was reported.

        Deriving from a normal AppCompat theme instead lets the platform build the
        starting window straight from android:windowBackground, i.e. the branded
        splash_window layer-list, so the native stage shows the logo and the
        loading bar and the two stages become one continuous splash.

        windowSplashScreenBackground / windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon /
        postSplashScreenTheme are deliberately gone: they only mean anything to
        Theme.SplashScreen, and on Android 12+ the platform reads
        windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon from the merged theme, so it is set on the
        application theme instead (see AppTheme) for those versions.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/splash_window</item>
        <item name="android:forceDarkAllowed">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>